            <ArticleTitle>Directed neurectomy for treatment of chronic postsurgical neuropathic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>246-8; discussion 248-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2012.10.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(12)00621-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Chronic neurogenic pain after surgery, especially inguinal herniorrhaphy, remains a major cause of morbidity. The traditional treatment of postinguinal herniorrhaphy neurogenic pain has included triple neurectomy with the removal of any mesh. This report describes a directed, minimally invasive surgical neurectomy that provided pain relief in 28 patients with minimal morbidity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">After temporary but successful proximal peripheral nerve blockade, the nerve was blocked in the operating room using a small amount of blue dye mixed with the local anesthetic. After confirming pain relief with the dye-anesthetic mixture, the patient was then sedated, and all blue-stained tissue was excised through a small incision, avoiding both the previous surgical scar and mesh.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All but 1 of the 28 patients had complete relief for a minimum of 12 months when discharged from follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This simple directed neurectomy method typically provides long-term relief for patients suffering from chronic postsurgical neurogenic pain.</AbstractText>
